inline.s:
The master’s dharma name was Zhuhong; his courtesy name Fohui, and his sobriquet Lianchi, Lotus Pond — the place toward which his aspiration turned. His secular surname was Shen; he was a man of Renhe in ancient Hangzhou, and his family had been a distinguished one for generations. His father was Shen Dejian, styled Mr. Mingzhai; his mother was of the Zhou family. From birth the master was bright and uncommon, and the flavors of the world were bland to him. At seventeen he entered the county school, and in examinations he repeatedly came first among the scholars; by his learning and conduct he was held in weight by his generation. The degrees were his for the taking, yet his will was set on leaving the world, and he often wrote the four words “the matter of birth and death is great” on the desk before him. Whenever he joined companions in discoursing on letters, he would always bend the talk back to Buddhist principle; already his heart rested in the Pure Land. His household abstained from killing, and their offerings were always vegetarian. Often he would sigh: “Human life passes like a white colt glimpsed through a crack. How much is there in this floating life? If by thirty I have not succeeded, I shall surely go beyond, and go for good. Why spend a lifetime on what is petty and squalid?” His first wife, of the Zhang family, bore a son who died young; then his wife died, and he wished not to marry again, but his mother forced him. A match was proposed with a woman of the Tang family, a poor girl who ate only vegetables. A wealthy man who wanted the master as his own fine son-in-law secretly worked to part them, yet the master took Tang in the end; but in his heart he did not wish to complete the rites of husband and wife. At twenty-seven his father died; at thirty-one his mother died. Then he wept and said, “My parents’ kindness is boundless; this is the very time to repay it.” By now his resolve to depart for good was fixed. In the yichou year of Jiajing, on New Year’s Eve, the master asked Tang to make tea. She carried it to the desk, and the cup cracked. The master laughed and said, “There is no union of causes and conditions that does not scatter.” The next year, bingyin, he took leave of Tang: “Love and affection are not lasting; birth and death none may bear for another. I am going. Plan for yourself.” Tang answered with spirit, “You go first; I shall follow at my leisure.” The master then composed the lyric A Stroke That Crosses It All Out, and straightway went to the monk Xingtian Li and had his head shaved. He sought the vinaya master Wuchen Yu of Zhaoqing Monastery and received full ordination at the platform. After a short while he set out with a single bowl and one staff to wander the regions, visiting good and wise teachers everywhere. Journeying north to Wutai, he was moved by Mañjuśrī’s emission of light. At Funiu he joined the assembly in their austerities. Entering the capital, he called on the two great elders Bianrong and Xiaoyan, and from each he received an opening. Passing through Dongchang, words suddenly came to him, and he made a verse: “Twenty years ago the matter was in doubt;
three thousand li away, what a strange encounter;
burning incense and casting down the halberd were all a dream —
demon and Buddha strive in vain over right and wrong.” Because his mourning for his mother was not yet ended, he carried her wooden tablet with him as he wandered; at every meal he made an offering, and wherever he stayed he served it. Such was his grief and longing. At Waguan Monastery in Jinling he fell so ill that he nearly died; his companions were about to carry him to cremation when the master faintly said, “I still have one breath left.” So they stopped. When the illness eased he returned. In Yue there were many Chan retreats; the master joined five of them, and to the end never knew the name of the monk on the neighboring seat. In the xinwei year of Longqing the master was begging in Fancun when he saw the quiet seclusion of Yunqi’s hills and waters, and formed the resolve to end his days there. The mountain was formerly the monastery of Chan Master Fuhu; Yang Guozhu, Chen Ruyu and others built him a thatched hut of three bays to dwell in. The master kept company with his own shadow on the cold cliff; once he went seven days without food, only sitting upright against the wall. The village had many tigers, and within forty li around the mountain no fewer than several dozen people were harmed each year; of all their troubles the people suffered most from this. The master roused his compassion and entreated on their behalf; he recited sutras and gave food offerings, and the tiger scourge was quieted. That year there was a severe drought, and the villagers begged the master to pray for rain. He laughed and said, “I know only Buddha-recitation; I have no other art.” The people insisted; unable to refuse, the master went out, and striking the wooden fish he walked along the fields reciting the Buddha’s name. Rain poured down at once, and fell wherever he went. The people marveled, and came in throngs, bringing timber and shouldering hoes and mattocks, vying to break ground there. They found a pillar base and pointed to it, saying, “This is a relic of the old Yunqi Monastery. The master has blessed our village; we wish to renew it, that our blessing may endure.” In a few days an aranya was finished — no lofty gate outside, no great hall within; only a Chan hall to settle the monks and a Dharma hall for the sutras and images, the rest serving merely to keep off wind and rain. From then on the Dharma flourished greatly; monks throughout the land turned their hearts to him, and it became a great monastery. The master grieved that in the age of the Dharma’s decline the net of the teachings was torn and broken, the way of Chan unclear, and beings’ karma deep and their defilements heavy — “to store sweet cream in a filthy vessel: this is what I fear.” Moreover, the Buddha established the three trainings to transform living beings, with the precepts as the foundation. If the foundation is not laid, on what do concentration and wisdom rest? Wishing to lead beings by what benefits them, one must first firm the root. But by the statutes of the realm the ordination platforms of north and south had long been banned and closed. Though he wished to revive the fallen discipline, how dared he defy the imperial decree? So he had the assembly recite the Brahma Net Precepts Sutra and the various precept chapters for monks every fortnight. Thereupon people near and far all turned to him. The master made strict observance of the vinaya his foremost practice, and wrote the Essential Outline for Novices, the Elementary Guide to the Full Precepts, and An Elucidation of the Brahma Net Sutra Commentary to make it clear. When the master first set out to travel and study, he gained power from investigating Buddha-recitation; by now he opened the single gate of the Pure Land, gathering all three capacities, and advocated it with all his strength. He therefore wrote the Commentary on the Amitābha Sūtra, more than a hundred thousand words, harmonizing the phenomenal and the principle and pointing all back to Mind Only. He also recalled having seen the recorded sayings of Gaofeng: in investigating this matter no one from of old had been keener or sharper — none surpassed this master, forged of pure steel. He had carried it with him on his wanderings. It was the master’s intent to unite this with Yongming of Mount Kuang in one. He further selected the most urgent sayings from the encounter dialogues of the ancient worthies and compiled them as Spurring On Through the Chan Gate, having it printed to show the secret of investigation. This reveals the dual cultivation of Chan and Pure Land, never departing from the one mind; thus one knows how subtle was the master’s skill in teaching. In the wuzi year of Wanli a great epidemic struck, and a thousand people died each day. The prefect Lord Yu Liangshu asked the master to go to Lingzhi Monastery to avert it, and the epidemic ceased. At Fancun there had once been the Zhu Bridge; the tides had washed it away, and travelers suffered in crossing. Lord Yu asked the master to take the lead in rebuilding it. The master said, “If you wish me to do it, then whether rich or poor, noble or base, each person shall give eight fen of silver and no more.” He used eight alone because the number eight is Kun, earth, and earth governs water. Someone said the work was great and the gifts small, and it would be hard to finish. The master said, “If the power of mind is great, the merit will of itself not decay.” In a few days a thousand gold pieces were amassed. Laborers were gathered and the foundation laid; for every pile driven he recited a mantra a hundred times, and for several days the tide did not come. The bridge was completed. Long ago King Qian shot at the tide with ten thousand crossbows; the master met it with the power of one mind. What art was this? The master’s standing in the Way grew daily, and monks from the ten directions came as if returning home. He received them all with kindness; disciples gathered daily and the dwelling grew cramped. The master did not care to adorn buildings but sought only what was peaceful and fit, mere shelters propped together. His pure regulations were set ever more strictly. The assembly had a common hall; those devoted to intensive practice, the aged and sick, and the visiting monks from the ten directions each had their own hall. Each of the hundred duties had its own quarters, every one with lock and key, opened and closed at set times. Each had its own words of warning, proclaimed at the appointed times. At night there were patrols; boards were struck and the Buddha’s name recited, the sound carrying through the valleys, so that even the weary could not sleep at ease and slept without dreams. At the fortnightly recitation and the karmic proceedings, merits and faults were brought up and rewards and penalties carried out, austere as ice and frost. Even when the Buddha dwelt at Jetavana there were still the six gangs troubling the assembly; here not one dared dispute or knowingly transgress. He did not bind himself wholly to Baizhang’s measuring line, yet he saved the ills of the age in good time; among monasteries ancient and modern there has never been one like this. All this is set out in the Monastic Regulations and the various admonitory texts, bright and clear. He was utterly intent on the precept against killing and honored the releasing of life; his writings on it long circulated in the world, and many within the seas honored and followed them. He once lectured on the Sutra of Perfect Enlightenment at Jingci Monastery; those who came to listen numbered thousands each day, ranged around him like a screen. He redeemed the Ten-Thousand-Labors Pond in front of the monastery as a life-releasing pond, and on the master’s eightieth birthday he enlarged it further. Today the two ponds, Shangfang and Changshou, in the city cost more than a hundred gold pieces a year. In the mountains a place for releasing life was established, where creatures that fly and walk were bought back and released until it was brimming; the monks cut down their own portions to feed them, at a yearly cost of about two hundred shi of grain. There were also words of warning for the keepers, proclaimed there at the appointed times. Even the feathered tribes, so fond of clamor, when they heard the sound of the wooden fish all fell silent and listened; when the recitation ended they beat their wings and sang aloud. Is this not Buddha-nature? Ah — the Buddha taught that filial piety itself is called precept, and the Confucians rebuked those who provide for parents without reverence. The master both provided for and revered living creatures, and did so with propriety: was this not filial piety brought to its fullest? The master’s way and style spread daily; the worthy and great within the seas, at court and in the countryside alike, all turned their hearts to him and were transformed. Such men as the Grand Marshal Lord Song Yingchang, the Grand Minister Lord Lu Guangzu, the Palace Admonisher Lord Zhang Yuanbian, the Director of Education Lord Feng Mengzhen, and Lord Tao Wangling came one after another to his gate to ask the Way, by the hundred; all knocked at the gate and beat time, inquiring into the great matter, and none failed to be won over, all entering his molding and forging. Provincial inspectors, prefects and ministers alighted from their carriages and bowed in salute, yet to the worthies and great men who came to pay their respects he gave no special courtesy and set out no feast. All were content with coarse rice and slept on worn mats, letting lizards crawl over them and mosquitoes bite without a change of countenance. All forgot their station and bent their power; those who came emptied themselves of what they had. Had his perfect sincerity not moved others, how could it have come to this? The Vice Minister Lord Wang Zongmu asked, “Last night a rat squeaked and squeaked and preached the whole Avatamsaka Sutra.” The master said, “What when the cat suddenly leaps out?” Wang had nothing to say, and the master answered for him: “The Dharma master runs off, and the lecture seat is left behind.” He also wrote a praise: “The rat squeaks and squeaks;
the Avatamsaka is clear and plain.
How strange is Vice Minister Wang —
deluded by a beast!
The cat springs out before the painted hall;
the bedside preaching leaves no word.
No word —
the Mahāvaipulya Buddhāvataṃsaka Sūtra, Chapter One, The Wondrous Adornments of the World Leaders.” The Censor Lord Zuo Zongying asked whether one could gain awakening by Buddha-recitation. The master said, “Turn the hearing to hear the self-nature; the nature accomplished, one attains the supreme Way. Why then doubt that turning the recitation back to recite the self-nature is the same?” The magistrate of Renhe, Lord Fan Liangshu, asked how a scattered mind could become still. The master said, “Set it in one place, and there is nothing that cannot be done.” A scholar in the assembly said, “To investigate one thing exclusively — that is setting it in one place. What can be accomplished by it?” The master said, “As for investigating things, one should simply follow Master Zhu’s sudden, thorough comprehension. What then cannot be accomplished?” Someone asked the master why he did not prize foreknowledge. The master said, “Suppose two men watch The Story of the Pipa. One has never seen it; the other has seen it and tells beforehand what will come. Yet they watch it through together — can he add or take away a single act?” The Emperor’s mother, the Empress Dowager Cisheng, revered the Three Jewels. Happening to see the master’s essay on releasing life, she greatly admired it, and sent palace attendants bearing a purple robe and funds for a vegetarian feast as offerings, and asked him for the essentials of the Dharma. The master bowed and accepted them, and answered with a verse. The master was utterly compassionate toward the suffering realms of the dark and unseen; he learned the flaming-mouth ritual himself and often performed it in person. Some once saw the Tathāgata’s form appear upon his seat — the power of his contemplation made it so. By nature the master was plain, simple and unadorned, without pretence; empty of heart, he responded to things. His countenance was gentle and pure, and he seemed too frail to bear the weight of his robes, yet his voice was like a great bell. His heart held no ramparts, yet he kept guard like a stern walled city and defended like steady troops. He was skilled at concealing his use; his patterns were fine and his scrutiny close; in ordering affairs great and small he let not a needle or a mustard seed slip by. Even in laying out the daily life of the monastery — measuring gifts and benefits, weighing what was fitting, checking causes and effects, making clear sin and merit, caring for the aged and sick, providing for the assembly of monks — not a drop of water was misdirected. In the fifty years since the monastery’s founding he never misspent a single coin. Though the resident community often numbered several thousand, he appointed no fundraiser, letting people come of themselves. Any surplus was at once distributed to other monasteries; the treasury held no savings. Whatever gold and silver came as offerings apart from the vegetarian feasts was scattered as soon as received, given for clothing and medicine, to relieve the poor and sick, with scarcely a day empty. Once he happened to examine his private accounts: in the last seven years he had in fact used more than five thousand gold pieces that did not belong to the permanent holdings — from which the years before may be known. Through life the master cherished his blessings, and once wrote thirty-two articles to warn himself. In old age he washed his own clothes and emptied his own chamber pot, not troubling an attendant. To the end of his life he wore plain cloth; a single hemp curtain, a thing from the days of his mother’s mourning, still survives. The rest may be known. Summing up the master’s conduct: with equal great compassion he gathered and transformed all. He spoke no word that was not the Buddha’s, did no deed that was not the Buddha’s, undertook no affair that was not the Buddha’s. The Buddha charged those who in the final age would protect the true Dharma to rely on the four practices of ease and bliss; the master truly lived by them. Looking through the patriarchs from of old: those who singly raised the true command did not necessarily cultivate all myriad practices. But as for one who manifests the one mind through the myriad practices, who sees the Buddha-nature within the dust of affliction — from ancient times to now, apart from Yongming, only the master alone. Earlier Confucians called Jiyin the Ban Gu and Sima Qian of monks; I would call the master the Zhou Gong and Confucius of the Dharma gate, for to bear the Dharma is to take up the Way. The master’s talent was enough to order the world, his awakening enough to transmit the mind, his teaching enough to fit the capacities at hand, his precepts enough to protect the Dharma, his conduct enough to rouse the age, his regulations enough to remedy its ills. As for kindness that gives joy and compassion that draws out suffering, and the wide practice of the six pāramitās — which of these is not a wondrous practice? From his leaving home to his end there was nothing to criticize; one may say that the Dharma gate gained one who embodied the Buddha’s whole substance and great function. Had he not been a great being of the manifestation body, lighting up the heavy darkness of the final age, how could he have come to this? At the end, half a month beforehand, he went into the city to take leave of his disciples and old friends, saying only, “I am going elsewhere.” Returning to the mountain, he came down into the hall again and again, set out tea and broth as offerings, and took leave of the assembly, saying, “I shall not stay here; I am going elsewhere.” At the mid-year festival the Ullambana basin was set out and each made offering for his ancestors. The master said, “This year I shall not join the gathering.” There was a register, and the master secretly wrote on it: “The duty monk of Yunqi Monastery makes offering on behalf of the late Master Lianchi of the hall, in memory of the Shen clan’s kin.” Only afterwards did they know that it was a sacrifice offered in advance. On the evening of the first of the seventh month he entered the hall and sat, charging the assembly: “You do not heed my words. I am like a candle in the wind; the lamp is out of oil. You will believe me only when I take one stumble and fall. Tomorrow I must go on a long journey.” The assembly urged him to stay. The master composed Three Regrets and Ten Lamentations to warn them. The layman Xu Lin of Songjiang and four others, five in all, were at the monastery, and he had an attendant deliver five copies of his testament. The following night he entered his quarters, showed a slight illness, and closed his eyes in silence. When his disciples from the city arrived and stood around him, he opened his eyes again and said, “All of you, recite the Buddha’s name honestly. Do not play the marvel-monger; do not ruin my regulations.” The assembly asked who should preside over the monastery. The master said, “One in whom precepts and practice are both complete.” They asked again about the present. The master said, “For now, follow the order of ordination.” Having spoken, he faced west, recited the Buddha’s name, and passed away upright. It was the noon hour of the fourth day of the seventh month in the forty-third year of Wanli. The master was born in the yiwei year of Jiajing; his worldly age was eighty-one, and his monastic years fifty. The master had himself chosen a spot below the ridge to the left of the monastery, and his whole body was enshrined there in a tower. His former wife, Madam Tang, afterwards shaved her head, founded the Xiaoyi Nunnery and became the head of a women’s monastery; she passed away one year before him, and her tower is also on the right-hand mountain outside the monastery. Among the master’s ordained disciples, Guangxiao and others were the foremost of the first generation. Those who came to his gate to receive precepts and ordination numbered no fewer than several thousand, not counting the laity. Officials and gentlemen who came to his gate likewise numbered in the thousands, not counting those who admired him from afar. His writings, apart from the sutra commentaries, include some twenty miscellaneous works such as the three volumes of Bamboo Window, all circulating in the world and all words of warning and awakening. The master always admonished his disciples to prize true practice and not to display marvels; therefore many of his spiritual wonders are not recorded here in full. Alas! I have heard that the World-Honored One, deeply mindful that living beings in the final age, though they be saved, might yet have their wisdom-life cut off, sought at the Vulture Peak assembly for those who would protect the true Dharma. Even those who had personally received the prediction dared not undertake it; only the host that sprang from the earth took it upon themselves. And they said, “We who uphold the sutra in the final age must possess great power of patience and great power of diligence. Even one who manifests a body here does not himself declare his origin or divulge the Buddha’s secret cause; only at the end, secretly, does he give a sign of it.” Looking at the master’s conduct — his hidden spirit and secret function, his power of patient endurance and diligence — is he not one of those who sprang from the earth? Or did he come from the Pure Land? Were it otherwise, then from the stage of an ordinary being, where even seeking one’s own benefit is not yet enough, how could one widely practice benefiting others and protect the true Dharma without defect from first to last? Moved by this, I have briefly gathered the master’s deeds to make them known to later ages; the rest is set out in the separate biographies. Thereupon I made this inscription, saying:

The essence of mountains and rivers makes culture shine;
The dharma's pulse flows hidden, clouds come, the valleys rush.
Here lies the spirit of She, long storing its wonder;
And so a wise one came and opened a single opening.
The dharma's teaching spread abroad, the mystic wind fanned each day;
In due season Master Zhu was born, his lofty standard bright as clouds.
A dragon of meaning in the ocean of teachings, a divine steed of the Chan school;
A single glance covers a thousand miles, lightning rolls, stars race.
He grasped the tally of going beyond, held the banner before words;
Hiding his radiance and veiling his light, he entered the boundless deep.
Embracing the Way and focusing spirit on green cliffs and emerald walls,
He sat and departed from them, his lone radiance blazing bright.
The stūpa's shadow props up the sky; the true wind brushes past;
The dharma-body stands majestic as clouds and mists come and go.

The master's monastic name was Zhiruo; his courtesy name was Yexi. He was a son of the Yao family of Shanyin; his mother was of the Yan family. She had first prayed to White-Robed Guanyin, and dreamed that a monk who was washing his feet told her, “I will make you a lion's cub.” She woke and was with child. He was keen and quick from birth, and as a child loved to sit cross-legged and recite the Buddha's name. His father died early and his mother lived in widowhood. He was only seven when his mother fell ill, and he wept bitterly day and night. When she was near death she charged him, saying, “You are a monk from a former life; do not betray your original vow.” With these words she passed away. Following his mother's dying command, the master sought out and bowed to Master Xian of Huayan Temple in Kuaiji and left home. At seventeen he was at last shaved and dyed. In his daily life he kept the great matter of birth and death close to heart, and he went straight to Niutou Mountain, resolving to investigate it. Before long he followed Master Jingshan and heard the Lotus Sutra expounded at Tiantai, and then hid himself in the mountains, straining with all his might at the matter of going beyond, dwelling alone for six years, until one day he touched a circumstance and had a breakthrough. At twenty-six he heard that Master Xuelang Hong'en had begun teaching in the southern capital, so with gourd and bamboo hat he set out. He first received full ordination from Master Su'an at Qixia, and then placed himself beneath Xuelang's seat, serving and studying for twelve years, exhaustively studying the scriptures and treatises and fathoming their profound mysteries. In the ji-yi year of Wanli, the Huihua Hermitage was founded at Zuili, and Sima Shen and Yue of the Ministry of Works invited the master to reside there. In the geng-yin year, when the master was thirty-six, Grand Minister Lu of Wutai, Assistant Censor Guan of Dongming, and Censor Liu Ziwei asked him to expound the Śūraṃgama Sutra at Wu Gate. In the ren-chen year he expounded the Lotus Sutra at Lingyin in Hangzhou, and the following year the Laṅkāvatāra Sutra at Jingci. In the ren-yin year he settled north of Feilai Peak in Wulin, where the old site of Yongfu Temple had fallen into private hands; Pan of the Court of Imperial Sacrifices redeemed and rebuilt it with a buddha-pavilion and a Chan hall, and so it became a hermitage. Throughout the three Wu and the two Zhe regions all took the master's teaching as their standard. At the monasteries where he stayed, he opened and expounded the scriptures and treatises in more than thirty places, and about fifty sessions in all, and he was called a master craftsman of his generation. The middle stretch of this record — the remainder of his later years, his death, and his disciples' request for this inscription — is corrupt in the source text. Of birth and death, firmly bound, there is none but is entangled;
How did the master see it? Thus, so free, so unconfined.
By seeds of prajñā, life after life perfumed and trained,
And so in coming and going he used no effort at all.
Moon of the precepts, flower of compassion,
Cloud of kindness, rain of dharma:
Benefiting self and benefiting others,
Pure and clean of any dust.
He fathomed the Buddha's mind, and spread his broad and long tongue;
Once the dharma's sound has reached the ear, its merit lasts for kalpas.
Alas, in this age of the dharma's decline, the master was a banner of the dharma;
May he long abide in the world, and demons and outsiders fall of themselves.
In birth and death, in coming and going, the dharma-body is still and quenched;
The master was truly unbound — this is true liberation.
The stūpa leans against a lone peak, and the sound of the pines never ceases;
Day and night, the perfect sound speaks on, blazing and ever constant.

In the mountain of the five aggregates, the ground of stillness and cessation;
the six windows stand open and empty, and light is born in the night.
You need only wake the master within the hut;
let him not cover and bury himself in dullness.

From Nanyue and Caoxi one vein descends;
the transmission says the bright mirror is no stand either.
The diamond eye of true seeing is shared by all;
it must be opened by that one phrase about grinding a brick.

The great matter of birth and death admits no bargaining;
to speak of it breaks the heart and severs the entrails.
Through countless kalpas we have let it slip by;
this time, how could we bear to fail the King of Emptiness?

The ocean assembly of the ten directions is my home;
in coming and going, the road I pass is never wrong.
Fragrant rice eaten to the full, I turn my head and go;
beyond the gate the misty waters stretch still farther without bound.

Gazing south from the hundred cities, all is misty waves;
to meet at a peak’s summit — what would that be like?
It is not that Sudhana lacked a face;
it is only that the teacher’s faith had gone astray.

Meeting beneath the tree was an old affinity;
since we parted, I have not counted how many lives ago.
Entering the gate, one smile, and the minds met;
only then did I believe that wordlessness is the secret transmission.

In the market, one room as cold as ice;
sitting cross-legged beneath the long-burning midnight lamp.
The Arhats come and go, and the seats are always full;
everyone knows that this is a monk who stays at home.

The great matter of birth and death deserves the deepest grief;
Set to work at once — even now you are already late.
Only seek your liberation within your own mind;
There is no need to go searching anywhere beyond it.

The Buddha said: In the presence of Dipankara Buddha I received no prediction in truth;
if there had been a prediction, that would be clinging to a self.
Even in becoming a Buddha one still fears clinging to a self — how much more in the affairs of birth and death.

Only wish to empty yourself of all that you have, and never fill yourself with what you do not have.
These words are not the private heirloom of Old Pang alone;
all Buddhas and patriarchs are the same.

When deluded thinking arises, nirvāṇa appears;
when afflictions stir, the Buddha Way is accomplished.
This dharma may be known and seen only by one whose five eyes are perfect and bright.

I have often said that learning has three essentials.
It is said: without understanding the Spring and Autumn Annals, one cannot engage with the world;
without mastering Laozi and Zhuangzi, one cannot forget the world;
without practicing Chan, one cannot leave the world.
With these three, the learning of ordering the world and of leaving it is complete.
Lack one, and you are one-sided; lack two, and you are narrow.
Lacking all three and still calling oneself a man — that is merely to resemble one.
Yet one must not fail to know the essential. The essential is the root.
Therefore it is said: Words have a root, affairs have a lord.
If words have no root, they sprawl without order;
if affairs have no lord, they fragment and grow ever more tangled.
If learning has no essential, it scatters and accomplishes little.
Hence the student must by no means fail to pursue the essential.
Yet the essential of these three lies in the one mind.
The essential of tending the mind lies in practicing Chan;
the essential of practicing Chan lies in forgetting the world;
the essential of forgetting the world lies in suiting the time;
the essential of suiting the time lies in comprehending change;
the essential of comprehending change lies in seeing principle;
the essential of seeing principle lies in steadying the will;
the essential of steadying the will lies in being content with one's lot;
the essential of being content with one's lot lies in curbing desire;
the essential of curbing desire lies in knowing oneself;
the essential of knowing oneself lies in valuing the true self;
the essential of valuing the true self lies in attending to what is within;
the essential of attending to what is within lies in singleness of focus.
Gain the one, and the principle of all under heaven is gained.
Following principle while engaging with the world, nothing is not forgotten, nothing is not possessed.
Forgetting nothing and possessing nothing, then of things none is not forgotten, none is not possessed.
When of things none is forgotten and none is not possessed,
then wherever one enters, one is at ease.
Therefore it is said: Heaven and earth are born together with me, and all things are one with me.
To gather all things into oneself — is this not the sage alone?
Ah, how perfect and complete! The wondrous is exhausted in the one mind, and no affair is left out.
Therefore the student surely must not fail to know the essential.

The mighty earth is a speck of dust,
The vast ocean a single drop.
Through the boundless reaches of space
All things give way and never stop.

The man of understanding takes the great view,
Looking up and down through a thousand ages.
All that has passed lies before his eyes;
Worthies and fools he can count on his fingers.

Beauty and ugliness are not worth naming;
Right and wrong — how can they be compared?
Confucius prized the knowing of Heaven’s decree;
Laozi valued the forgetting of self.

But our Great Hero, the World-Honored One,
Rises beyond birth and death altogether.
The world is a bubble on the water;
Body and mind are but dregs of dust.

Illusion and transformation are only thus;
Honor and disgrace — what grief or joy?
Let the inverted mind take empty flowers as real;
This is all I see of it.

A little boat carries the bright moon,
Racing along with the rushing stream.
The sail’s shadow seems to shift and turn;
The moonlight neither comes nor goes.

Mind and surroundings are at root still and void;
Where then are death and birth?
Wherever one dwells is lasting joy;
All beyond is dust and dirt.

Its transformations have no beginning or end;
Its breathing makes the clouds and rain.
Its rich bounty moistens all living things;
Its pouring torrent soaks the land below.

In a flash it fills the ninefold heavens;
In a moment it covers all the world.
How unlike the worm within the sand,
That rots in stench along with other things.

A long roar issues and the mountain is empty;
A mournful wind shakes all the trees.
Rustling, cold comes the autumn rain;
Wailing, the night ghosts weep.

Its rank and bloody smell it flaunts in vain;
Its hide and fur are fit to be worn.
How much better the peace of the burrowing mole,
That drinks from the river and is content with a full belly.

You came toward Caoxi,
Straight along the Caoxi road.
By the stream I suddenly met you,
And at first sight it was as of old.

One smile, and eye and mind open;
Host and guest forget the forms of courtesy.
We sit knee to knee deep into the night,
Unfolding clearly our inmost hearts.

Your high resolve is bright as snow and ice;
Your pure words ring like gold and jade.
You gaze down and the six directions are empty;
You set out upon a thousand-mile road.

At the year’s end you go on a far journey;
The principle is dark — there is no going or staying.
I take your hand and see you off;
Alone on the bridge I linger.

Mount Wutai in the three hottest weeks;
Jiangnan in the twelfth-month trees.
A lone trace is a cloud in the sky;
My remaining life is dew on the grass.

Cold and heat have no beginning or end;
North and south, I let them go or stay.
Wherever I may be there is coolness enough;
What else is there here to long for?

In youth I was steeped in the will to wander far;
Long have I admired the master of Donglin.
He opened the White Lotus Society in the green hills,
And all the high worthies were gathered there.

They treasured the hours and set the lotus water-clock,
Purifying themselves and bowing at the six periods.
Their pure mindfulness cut off all dusty thought;
The Pure Land was their appointed home.

Their high wind has stirred a thousand years;
I lift my head and knit long thoughts.
Their shining countenances seem before my eyes;
In dreams I follow after them.

Only in declining years did I climb up here,
And touching the scene my sorrow grows.
Wild woods shade the fallen walls;
Rank grass grows thick again.

I linger where the three men smiled;
Moss and lichen show their bloom.
In the shadow hall the host of worthies stands,
And I seem to see their fragrant rule.

The old steps are locked in cold mist;
White lotuses open in the muddy pool.
Fragrant Valley sends out clear echoes;
The pipes of earth are blown by the wind of heaven.

Hills and mounds may shift and change;
The ultimate Way knows no alteration.
The masters had a vow unfinished —
Why should they be slow to come again?

If the grove were opened as of old,
Their lofty traces could still be followed.
The mountain spirit has long protected it;
The sacred power is still here.

I am ready to await my end;
From now on I take leave of the muddy world.

Since I parted from you, thousands of miles;
My longing for you runs on like flowing water.
The water races east and does not return;
My heart is like a ring with no end.

I lift my head and gaze at the long sky;
The long sky is endless and far.
I wave my hand and invite the bright moon;
The bright moon has its time of coming.

Though light and shadow may look at one another,
They can be watched but never reached.
If only your face were like the full moon,
That one look might open my heart.

Hou Sheng’s poverty shames the guests of the Yi Gate;
Who comes to his house with bridle in hand?
He alone has thoughts of painting that enter the spirit;
His elegance is fit to be called utter folly.

His one room hangs empty, clear as snow and ice;
Mist and cloud rise at times from his pores.
Deer and elk run to and fro in the empty air;
Mountain spirits start in fright night after night.

The days are long; he shuts his door and sweeps his yard;
Fine meat is not enough, mist and sunset glow fill him.
He soaks his ink and takes it for ambrosia;
The people of the time wonder at his looks.

For many years now, through the sixty-year cycle,
He has spent half of it in constant hunger.
A foot of cloth, a peck of grain, traded for good wine;
Clear spring and white stone make men mock him.

Sometimes he stands alone at the street corner;
Seeing people, before he speaks he is ashamed.
Everyone says a poor man’s bones are soft as mud;
Who would believe his heart is hard as iron?

The three rivers and five lakes roll in vast waves;
A thousand cliffs and ten thousand ravines contend in strange shapes.
Idly he spreads the silk, lightly wields the brush,
And gathers them all at once on his brow’s tip.

He goes into the mountains seeking out stumps and snags;
Beneath the pines, at one meeting, they call out in joy.
Drinking tea, he regrets a thousand cups are too few;
Asking after Dharma, he has never had half a word.

Old Hou has a big belly and a long tongue;
He swallows and spits the light of the moon for ten thousand miles.
The stars scatter down and no one gathers them;
You, sir, are fit to be my ode-bag.

Let me keep you for now, lying in the mountains,
With white clouds for a canopy and the blue sky for a tent.
When thirsty, drink the clear stream, chew the purple mushrooms;
Let the sea dry and the stones rot — who cares?

In former days I roamed the land of wondrous adornment,
And saw the udumbara flower, white as snow.
Beneath the flowers stood rows of great beings,
Their countenances shining with golden light.

This flower does not open at any common time;
Once in a thousand years it forms its bud.
When a true man is about to appear, the flower blooms first —
It comes alone for the great matter of conditions.

The great matter of conditions is not of one kind;
A hundred thousand million bodies all save the world.
The three vehicles and eight assemblies are answered as thought arises;
The dharma-realms of the ten directions come as the mind wills.

Sometimes he manifests the body of a great heavenly general,
His awesome bearing on every side like a god of heaven.
He sweeps across ten thousand miles, all but unmatched;
The comet is wholly swept away and the smoke and dust cleared.

His transformations have no beginning or end, most rare;
They are like the udumbara opening in a smile.
Playing the flute beneath the moon, the phoenix comes;
Brandishing the spear on horseback, the lion roars.

Once opened, it becomes a portent for the world;
The man and the flower-spirit are not two.
The man imitates Jetavana’s mind of spreading the ground;
The flower becomes the garden’s work of merit.

The general does not depart from Chan in the use of arms;
His small hermitage is built beside Jetavana.
Facing the flowers, his mind enters the land of no-birth;
Shutting his door, his body dwells in the heaven of Ultimate Bliss.

The killing intent is wholly the wisdom that subdues demons;
Color and fragrance wondrously reveal the meaning of the Patriarch’s coming.
Seeing form, smelling scent, the dharma-realm is empty;
On the spot, in battle, he enters samadhi.

The flower’s master moves in the rich creative force;
His literary brilliance runs free and surpasses ancient and modern.
His springtime decrees send forth thunder;
Wind and cloud change their form and drive dragons and tigers.

The garden is vast, and the flowers are unharmed;
The hermitage is just one fathom square.
Ancient and modern, heaven and earth are empty within a mustard seed;
The world, its mountains and rivers, is an image in a mirror.

The udumbara in the mirror is doubly full of spirit;
The general too is a man within the mirror.
Ten thousand miles hang in the void like a face before him;
How can the eye hear as well as the ear sees truly?

I too am a historian beneath the Jetavana flowers,
Watering them always with the water of dhyana.
The creeping weeds of the five aggregates were long ago cut down;
The four elements, this phantom body, have no support.

I cast it out to the burning wilds like a proclamation;
Cauldron of boiling water and furnace of coals meet no resistance.
Suddenly I saw the old master among the flowers;
Send word: do not forget the road by which you came.

Withered-Wood Hut, Withered-Wood Hut,
Before Elephant Ridge, the lion’s lair.
Its gnarled branches twist beyond all climbing;
Frosted leaves spread through the clouds in tangled strands.

Looked at from afar, it seems a stump not worth the taking;
Approached, it is withered and dry as true investigation.
If one set this in Old Man Xue’s hall,
How many Manjusris, before and behind?

I am already old and decayed, my sea of knowing dried;
Let me sit with you in the Withered-Wood Hut.

The burning house blazes and the dream is not yet woken;
Within the dust there is one realm of coolness.
One sees only the raging flames mounting the sky,
While the man of the Way keeps a single thought like a frozen well.

The market’s din roars like ten thousand galloping horses;
Day and night it never rests — for what?
His ear-root is stilled and the mind does not arise;
All of it, he sees, is falsehood in the air.

Wife and children face him like phantom men;
He looks back and sees himself as a shape in a mirror.
All day they meet and do not know one another;
Where do his two eyes find room for a speck of dust?

Sometimes his spirit roams the realm of the lotus treasury,
And one sweep of the brush releases the block within his breast. (Langqu is skilled at painting.)

The marshes of Chu are not the burning southern border;
Walking and chanting, I am ashamed to be the only sober one.
Miasmic smoke blackens a thousand peaks;
Last year’s grass is green in all four seasons.

The hurricane strikes and the autumn waves rage;
Men shrink before the fierce ghost’s power.
From of old all has been drifting traces;
And today I drift still more like duckweed.

Who can escape the burning house?
Coolness can be sought of oneself.
The sky hangs low, near the sun;
The trees are old and do not know autumn.

How still the mind before the sea’s moon;
The empty clouds seem about to float.
Yet I pity the guest who dwells nowhere —
Today he lodges again in the burning isle.

Of old they spoke of the Leiyang road;
Now I pass west of Dianbai.
Ten thousand mountains close in their mist;
One staff is lost in the miasmic smoke.

On the last stretch I follow the tumbling weeds;
This remnant life trusts to the horse’s hooves.
How can I bear it, deep in the trees,
Where the partridge cries on every side?

On the long road where I walk,
A lone cloud is all I can lean on.
Whoever has a home is still a guest;
Whoever has no ties is a monk.

Poisonous mist fumes till the mind is drunk;
The burning wind steams through the bones.
I turn and think of the places I once roamed —
They seem like treading on layer on layer of ice.

Wandering was always my own affair;
To shoulder my umbrella is well within my power.
My heart hangs like the moon for ten thousand miles;
My shoulder bears a single staff of rattan.

In eating I am troubled by the barbarous tongue;
In sitting in Chan I delight in common monks.
Subduing demons — in vain one speaks of the sword;
Today, at last, I take the foremost way.

Though I have left the world, I still march on campaign;
Who pities the hardship of the road?
A long spear serves me for a staff;
My close-cropped hair will not bear a cap.

I drink the evening mist of the three isles;
The burning heat plagues a hundred barbarous tribes.
Where I turn my head in the southern sky,
The setting sun there is Chang’an.

Heaven above covers all things;
Why should it shut out the distant wilds?
Twisting and turning, my life is short;
The roads of the world are long to ride.

I only know my mind is like snow;
Suddenly I feel my temples are like frost.
Anywhere on earth a man may bury his bones;
How can I forget the sovereign’s grace?

Once I dwelt in the realm of coolness;
Now I climb the heaven of burning affliction.
The air of warmth and cold is different;
North and south, the land lies atilt.

Ten thousand miles share the same bright moon;
A thousand mountains are parted by dark mist.
Though the frontier goose may carry a letter,
It will not pass beyond Wild-Goose Peak.

From my childhood I followed the pure karma;
In declining years I laid aside the black robe.
Can it be that the sovereign’s grace was thin?
It must be that the world’s way runs contrary.

My luggage of mist and sunset glow is little;
Snow and ice are seldom before my eyes.
Do not ask about the road ahead;
Home hills are found wherever I am.

At dawn I rise and divine the weather;
The river of stars parts the dawn light.
The tide swallows the red phoenix sun;
The mountains spit out the poisonous dragon cloud.

Drifting, I am companion to the gulls;
Resting, I remember the herd of deer.
Who knows that this fugitive from the world
Learns at his life’s end to follow the army?

On this day, on the road to the sky’s edge,
In hardship I can only pity myself.
The sea wind stinks and brews rain;
The mountain air is poisonous and holds smoke.

On the dreaded road I follow behind others;
Stumbling through mud I press on before the horse.
Only now do I know the bitterness of marching,
Mostly on the frontier soldier’s side.

Lodging on the road, I grieve at the Cold Food Festival;
Weapons and war have aged the years.
After passing through nine deaths,
My mind is the state before birth.

For the northern campaign I think of Shanfu;
For the southern march I remember Ma Yuan.
From what place comes the plum-blossom flute?
I hear it through and cannot sleep

With one bowl I follow the army;
On a lone march I drift like a tumbleweed.
This bodily frame rides the wild horses of the air;
My heart’s affair is entrusted to the dark goose.

The clouds rise white over Cangwu;
The sunset glow steams red on the sea’s sun.
My life was long ago cast away;
I need not wait for this hour to be empty.

In this floating world I gladly am a guest;
I resent this body that toils through life.
So long as the tongue remains, there is suffering;
Since the Way is here, how can I be called poor?

Thirsty deer race toward the mirage flame;
Hungry crows grow used to coming near men.
Though the seas and fields have not yet changed,
What place is there where dust does not fly?

One breath is the surplus of this remnant life;
The road away through a thousand mountains is long.
Asking the way, I meet a horseherd;
Clutching a book, I pair with the man who lost his sheep.

My heart burns as in the three autumn days;
My mind is cold as the sixth-month frost.
Wherever I pass is like treading a cauldron;
How dare I give free rein to wildness?

My phantom traces have never dwelt anywhere;
Meeting a mountain, I should go home at once.
Because I see the road ahead grow narrow,
I see this life as slight.

Often I bear the hunger of the man beneath the mulberry;
Always I cherish the starving washerwoman’s need.
What I rejoice in is that there is no twelfth month here,
So I do not look for winter clothes to be sent.

Mingling with the world, I hope to forget its forms;
Facing the troops, I think of herding rams.
Before me I drive the grass of patience;
Baring one shoulder, I twist my robe askew.

The setting sun makes the river seem drunk;
The homeward clouds confuse the color of the trees.
My going and staying are like a weary bird’s,
Lowering little by little toward men.

Leopards and tigers fill the central plain;
The star-bearer’s coach races day and night.
The decree holds no word of grief or pity;
Men sit with their faces to the wall in sorrow.

Far off they probe the black dragon’s lair;
Deep they split the weak tree’s branch.
Heaven and earth — for a while I bow and lift my head;
My grief is utter in these two brows.

At Qinghai a victory was just won;
At Zhuyai the campaign was first called off.
Red-feathered dispatches fly through Sword Gate;
Qiang soldiers run along the plank roads.

The emperor’s ear favors the winning of distant peoples;
The royal army is ashamed of war.
The barbarians should be bound with long ropes —
Yet no one asks for the long cord.

Yellow dust darkens all that meets the eye;
My shorn hair hangs down over my shoulders.
The homeward road through rivers and lakes is faint;
Gulls and herons eye a man with doubt.

Of relief and succor I think today;
For safety and peril I look to this hour.
From of old the sable and jade ear-ornaments carry weight —
How can one not be ashamed before such grace?

Ten thousand ravines come rushing down;
A thousand peaks join purple and green.
The sail flies through the Three Gorges rain;
A man enters the ninth-autumn sky.

The traveler’s road lies beyond the floating clouds;
The homeward heart rests before the setting sun.
This life of mine is not yet over;
The rivers and Han are my remaining years.

Upstream I follow the far line of shoals;
My journey moors beside a lonely pavilion.
The moon withdrawn, the mountain's face grows pale;
The fish dive deep, the water's breath is rank.
Grass-insects chirr along the broken bank;
Sand-birds roost on the cold and barren isle.
Most do I pity him who drifts and falls —
This floating life, its dream not yet awake.

A half couch serves the stillness of my Chan;
On hand-propped cheek I lie in white cloud air.
Its humble, hollow heart dotes on me still;
Its lofty joints — for you alone I care.
Wiped clean, it keeps the Xiang queens' tearful stains;
Cut fine, it weeps the patterns of old Chu.
Best it can soothe the sickness of my bones;
From this day forth I bar all worldly hue.

Through many wars this body has grown old;
In wind and smoke my temples show their gray.
My bones are tired and shun the iron mail;
My heart grown cold, I love the green hills' way.
On wooden slips, the taste of Chan, detached;
In tea's sweet fragrance all affairs grow still.
Gladly with the white clouds I make my home —
Who would let me pass the pine-gate sill?

A single stream winds inward to the vale;
The crowding peaks turn back on either shore.
One thinks the folk still live as in Qin's day;
The monks seem come from India's ancient shore.
Bamboo and trees grow lengthening into cloud;
The fields unfold as spreads the land below.
Who knows, within the Five Ranges' winding turns,
A Tiantai of its own is set to show?

A hundred feet from one small sprout arise;
A single root thrusts up through weeds and brush.
It has endured the smoke and miasma's grief;
Has heard the crane's lament cry out and hush.
Put to great use, it should not come to harm;
Cut to a shape, how should that be a woe?
I only grieve for tonight's moon, that shines
In vain upon the mosses here below.

A fragrant shrine floats on the hidden sea;
A flowered banner lifts a Brahma hall.
Green spiral shows the Buddha's jeweled hair;
The full moon shows his kindly face to all.
The world lies level as a palm outspread;
The river's current cleans to empty space.
Pity the hunted ones who run and strive:
They all fall within the dharmakāya's grace.

Upon this wild road near and far are one;
Its windings double every stage I go.
The land turns with the river as it runs;
Men walk a path that only birds can know.
A lone duck vanishes among the clouds;
A single sail hangs light at treetops' ends.
I turn my head toward the road of Chang'an:
No frontier goose-song to my ear descends.

I urge my horse and watch the post-house near;
This long road I have traveled many a year.
All day the mountain vapors drift in white;
In the tenth month the burned ground greens aright.
My face burns hot with betel-nut's hot wine;
My spirit dulls in sea-fog's rank and brine.
From the lone town the horn and drum arise —
So grim, so grand, I cannot bear their cries.

The waning moon hangs on the city wall;
The bugle deepens a traveler's sorrow.
The north wind blows about my thinning hair;
Cold dew soaks through my heavy fur.
Wild burnings run along the ramparts' line;
Frontier beacons dim the watchtower's face.
A lonely cloud stands pale, then drifts away —
At ease at last, floating in empty space.

Ridges coil away across the vastness;
Their windings leave no count of fold or tier.
The road on either hand seems Hangu Pass;
The people seem of Wuling's hidden mere.
Deep in the woods the tigers and leopards hide;
Far in the sky the falcon stoops to strike.
And why upon this windy, dusty way
Must I, an old monk, drive and urge all day?

Alone I travel beyond ten thousand li;
The farther the road, the deeper grows my sigh.
The hills wear all the vapor of the python;
The speech of men is birds' strange chattering.
A wayside hut is all today's affair;
Ice and snow, the heart of a whole life.
Though trees that pierce the sky are growing here,
They are no Jeta Grove, to saints most dear.

I pass out through a barren valley's mouth;
Above the treetops see the world's far end.
The wild is wide and keeps the lingering light;
The ruined town is girt with sunset's red.
Urged on by hunger, I forget my weariness;
To hurry more, I count the long road still.
At dusk I lodge within a mountain inn —
I sleep as soundly as at home, at will.

The hills bow low and plunge into the gorge;
A single stream comes winding to the village.
The level plain unfolds the burning frontier;
Along the border garrison camps lie.
The people's lives waste all their years in vain;
The seasons lose their cold, their warmth forget.
Say not the world's end lies too far away:
Fusang is near the dawn of coming day.

The long road wearies all the strength of foot;
The broad plain opens out before my eyes.
The land here ends at the Southern Sea;
Pearl luster links the Dipper in the skies.
Far hills lie low and merge into the trees;
The great sea stands level with the sky.
It looks like clouds with drooping wings outspread —
Sails spread, the swift treasure-ship goes by.

In this hot land the seasons strange appear;
The year's affairs are hard to reckon clear.
The twelfth month ends, yet insects do not hide;
Spring comes, and birds know nothing it has come.
Bean-blossoms open on the old bean-pods;
Banyan leaves fall from the newest bough.
And so I think of Yan Mountain's snows —
The kindly warmth seems partial to them now.

Over the Qiong Sea the spring clouds gather deep;
The steamy heat lies sunk in lingering mist.
The orchid's fragrance turns to fevered air;
The thorny bamboo grows to forests' grief.
Ask nothing of the labors of this life;
Look only at this heart that nears its death.
I tire of beard and hair, this heavy load —
Day after day I long to draw the pin.

Autumn waters brim with lotus flowers;
A little boat, one leaf of slender weight.
On the still stream I am in my old house;
To drift abroad is but to journey home.
A scattered rain drives back the steamy heat;
A clear wind lifts the ripples like crepe's crease.
I come and go wherever I am inclined;
The partridge's “Go not!” I pay no mind.

On either bank the hills let fall their shade;
A thousand peaks pour upside down through space.
Among the clouds flies Vulture Peak away;
Beneath the waves the dragon palace shows.
In fine descent the heavenly flowers rain;
Long sounds the wind, the music of the earth.
The racing current takes my boat in tow —
For one brief while it will not let me go.

Cold rain comes sprinkling on my brushwood door;
The chilly sound by little and little dies.
At first I think it but a lean plum's shade;
I scarce believe that snow can fly at all.
It presses heavy on the plantain leaves;
It lightly mocks the creeping fig's green cloak.
Eight years my dreams have labored toward this sight;
Today I joy to see the falling light.

Bearing my pack through ten thousand li;
Seven years I tarried in the burning land.
I chose my dwelling by the Ganges' bank;
My heart was drawn by one long stream outspanned.
Having a body, I can bear its load;
Having no thing, no thoughts need I compose.
Before the cliff where the cut arm's tree stands,
Once more I breathe the cassia's fragrance close.

The great earth floats upon the fragrant sea;
A lonely spire lifts like a Brahma banner.
In sky and water Vulture Peak appears;
In caves of fire the poison dragon yields.
Sun and moon hang like mirrors in the void;
Heaven and earth glow in a night-lamp's light.
I gaze at clouds and flick my five fingers free —
A rain of flowers falls at the empty window's height.

The general's hall stands cold and desolate;
Here I lodge, a banished guest, and wait.
Too lazy, I let worldly matters go;
More idle than the white clouds, drifting slow.
I wave my whisk, too slack to tame the deer;
Beside the pool I learn the goose-lover's cheer.
In some deep valley, hidden from the day,
Could it be better? That I cannot say.

The white sun burns as fierce as fire can be;
I lie and sleep; the night air chills me through.
Waking from dream, I come back to the moon's cave;
My thoughts go down into an icy bowl.
At the camp gate, drums and horns at dawn;
The River of Stars fades in the morning pale.
Awake, I let my loosened hair hang free —
I grieve to see the bamboo-skin cap on me.

These pond waters bring lake and river thoughts;
The fishes' joy I have not yet forgot.
Forever I remember the great gulf;
My secret mind rests on the Hao bridge's way.
The new moon sinks — a slender, bending hook;
The willow trails a long and silken line.
Tonight the wind and rain will rise and shake —
How the scaled fins will leap and flash and shine!

Easily I put off dust's entanglements;
Hard to dissolve this body of great woe.
In act or rest I mingle with the crowd;
But age and sickness spare no man, I know.
Ox teeth and horse teeth soon will be worn down;
The ape's and monkey's mind is not yet pure.
My six sense-faculties hold their own domains —
I cannot tell which one is kin and sure.

A narrow path, a hillside's slender waist;
The clear stream's ribbon stretches long and low.
The pine-cones fall to meet the facing wind;
Dew-soaked, the rice-bloom sends its fragrance up.
Village roofs are green as lotus buds;
The houses stand with walls of whitened board.
If you have never come to Peach Blossom Spring,
Then ask no fisherman to be your guide.

At dusk I come down from the lofty peak;
Deep in the hills the heat lies thick and still.
The air steams under dog-day sun and burns —
Then coolness sprinkles over a pine-clad hill.
The rushing wind drives on the chilling rain;
Rank-smelling clouds wake dragons where they lie.
I hurry home to lodge in Huazang's walls;
The dream breaks — the upper bell sounds from the sky.

A single reach leads on to the fragrant sea;
A thousand peaks about the phantom city crowd.
The blue lotus unfolds its slender leaves;
The moon of wisdom shines aloft and bright.
The world far off, all conditions come to rest;
The mind at leisure, the five defilements light.
If but one mote of dust may be broke through,
Here and now the unborn is realized.

A sudden rain drives off the burning heat;
The new-come autumn breathes a crisper air.
The bank's sand disappears beneath the flood;
The river moon keeps company with me.
Gathered foam is but the toil of living;
Floating clouds, a passing traveler's heart.
Beside the stream I watch the bubbles' gleam —
And see this body turn to something light.

One leaf goes riding off upon the wind;
The little boat goes homeward with the stream.
The hills coil round like ants upon the ground;
The river winds about me like a ring.
This body merges with the empty clouds;
The mind lies idle with the moon on the water.
Ten thousand peaks — I lie down safe at home;
In silence I close up the pine-wood gate.

The setting sun is lovelier when it clears;
The homeward road grows cold and longer drawn.
An idle heart finds clouds that never weary;
The birds must know the tiredness I bear.
The roads of the world will never find an end;
This toiling life must have a term somewhere.
I turn my head among ten thousand peaks:
Who chews the purple-stemmed herb on those steeps?

An empty boat goes where it will go;
One stretch of water ends the passes' woe.
The moon looks lovelier the more I gaze;
The river's voice grows quieter as I hear.
Floating clouds are matters outside the self;
White hair is but the mirror's image here.
Say not that I have drifted far and long;
The way ahead is my own mountain's song.

Not tarrying, I am a guest from the first;
An empty boat, a tumbleweed that rolls.
On either side the river, cliffs a thousand feet;
Half sail in rain, and half a sail of wind.
It skims the rocks as swallows skim the air;
It rides the current as on emptiness.
The ancient meaning of the ferry lost —
It lies in every coming and every going.

Drop after drop, the heart has lost its thread;
Thread after thread, the mood grows skilled in pain.
A single boat is lost by distant shores;
Two eyes grow dim across the long, wet sky.
The trees of a thousand villages are gone;
Still blows the wind through ten thousand hollow places.
Grief adds itself to the newly gathered waters —
Rolling and rolling in the rushing stream.

In old age I would lean on quiet grace;
My truest hope a grove where Dharma grows.
A hundred years are gathered in tonight's talk;
Kalpas on kalpas meet in this hour's mind.
The snow lies white across the Hengshan's back;
The clouds lie deep upon the Xiang's dark stream.
To rest and have done — that now is all my aim;
No more will I spend strength on seeking's dream.

A heart that wearies of the world grows fixed;
How shall it bear when sickness plays the fiend?
I know already that my days are few;
And still this body seems the more to grow.
Medicine and stone fill up the fragrant bowl;
My very groaning is my karma-rite.
If Mañjuśrī should come and question me —
One silent pause: what answer could be right?

Spring rain has passed across the Xiao and Xiang;
A light sail hangs in morning's frosty air.
The racing current turns at Stone Drum's height;
The risen water crosses through Hengyang.
The marchmount's color seems to draw quite near;
The lake's far sky stretches away in length.
Who knows that in this sea of dust and pain
A ford, a bridge, are found in every place?

The setting sunlight soaks the lake and sky;
The sand is bright, the moon is in the boat.
Birds roost in trees that lean above the water;
Voices speak beyond the wood's thin smoke.
In this floating world I lodge a single night;
This remnant life — how many years can be?
Why is it, in my lean and fading days,
I am still held by Chu's riverside to stay?

The lake is shallow, and not hard to cross;
Yet the wind's sail is not so lightly set.
The sheep-gut sands wind crooked as they run;
The bird-feather banks lie ragged and uneven.
The land bends round, two turning wheels in flight;
The empty sky hangs like a single mirror.
I look back at the place where I must moor —
The new moon shines on Moth-Eyebrow, and clearer.

The many mountains join the earth's own veins;
The many waters pour to Heaven's Heart.
Vast and measureless, the world is wide;
Sun and moon float and sink in depths apart.
The sail flies with a solitary bird;
My gaze goes out into the level wood.
If I should follow where the small boat goes —
In mist and wave, where could I then be found?

The whitewashed walls are hid in morning glow;
The lonely town stands by the water's side.
Along the dike the willows spread their green;
About the wall the peach trees blossom wide.
Far in the sky a yellow swan takes flight;
The clear stream runs with sand of whitest stone.
I know that Wuling cannot be far off —
At the ferry I see a fisherman's home.

His crown possessed the vajra's diamond eye;
His breast concealed a chestnut burr of thorn.
A single phrase went off like thunder's crack;
From all four seas the heroes came, and ran.
The patriarch's intent — offered before the trigger;
All common feeling — emptied where it stands.
The Chan gate's born slayer of the self —
Awesome, one sees the true wind in his hands.

My hair is not so white as is my heart;
My form is like a withered, leafless tree.
All conditions end in the place of rest;
One single thought, seen through, is left alone.
Heaven already pardons my crude slowness;
True Chan is free of being and of non-being.
My remnant life is like the setting sun —
And every step I take is homeward road.

This body is no longer safe to trust;
Its broken parts each have their reason why.
For a while the four great elements join;
At last they gather into specks of dust.
Ten thousand sounds hold emptiness and quiet;
All conditions show the original truth.
Always and always, amid sound and form,
How many people have been led astray.

A hut no bigger than a bushel measure —
Within it I let roll and unroll at pleasure.
Clouds and bright mists rise from door and window;
Stars and the moon hang on the courtyard stair.
Thoughts stilled, the mind grows quieter and quieter;
Dust gone, the world of things is as it is.
The southern breeze comes sometimes to my seat;
Rustling, the six windows stand empty and clear.

When I have eaten, I have no other task;
I sleep so long I know not day from night.
Of dark and light I scarcely am aware;
How often have I heard the bells and drums?
On every side blue peaks are curtains hung;
Together with my body I lie in white cloud.
Who says that tea has power and vigor in it,
Able to rout the armies of sleep's god?

I have no heart for this world of men;
My spirit roams in the Heaven of Utmost Bliss.
There stays but this old vessel full of leaks;
I am ashamed to deal in blind-man's Chan.
I envy only wings that beat the wind;
I prize the lotus rising from the water.
I look back at the guests who dwell in dust —
Who is not tangled, sleeping, and asleep?

The Xiang's waters run to Ba and Han;
A lonely sail goes into Chu's wide sky.
A patch of cloud lies low on distant trees;
The clear sun shines upon the slanting stream.
To live in this world is always to be a guest;
Of this floating life, ask not how many years.
Even if I follow the road that leads me home,
It is still like a boat that waits at the ferry.

At dawn I leave the clear pool's winding bend;
I raise my sail and trust the water's flow.
The sail goes flying after birds in flight;
The banks turn with me as the boat moves on.
The trees are far — one thinks the sky ends there;
The river's empty — see the earth afloat.
Lake Dongting lies, it seems, a foot away;
And step by step I near Yueyang's tower.

The boat lies moored among the reeds for the night;
At break of day I pass by Jiayu's wall.
The hills come out — the town looks small above;
The river holds the trees' sparse shadows all.
The sky hangs low — I think the sun is near;
The water reaches far — as on the void I ride.
A single reed goes off upon the wind,
Drifting wherever winds and waters guide.

Seeking out beauty, I traveled Xiao and Xiang;
Riding the current, I passed by Wuchang.
Rivers and hills make Hankou's grandeur stand;
Clouds and rain once deceived the King of Xiang.
Far off, the tracks of the flying yellow crane;
A light sail hangs against the setting sun.
This life goes drifting with the departing water —
No need to ask if I should stay or run.

Seven marshes command Jing and Xiang;
one long water joins with the sky.
The river’s flow turns back at Red Cliff;
the hills’ hues embrace Huanggang.
For rhapsodies, the Fisherman is praised;
in walking songs, Chu’s madman is recalled.
I have always longed to wash my feet —
and now I am already in the Canglang.

This mountain was my dwelling in a former life;
this forest opens from today.
Erring, I was snared by dust and toil and went away;
rejoicing, I come back by grace of an old bond.
Bones stand stark as these lean ridged peaks;
mind at leisure, a hundred thoughts turned ash.
Mist and cloud are now enough for me —
why should I ask after Mount Tiantai?

Fleeing the world brings no gloom at heart;
dwelling in mountains, I never tire of depth.
Thick clouds bring rain though the sky is clear;
in hidden ravines the day keeps its shade.
Fawns sleep in the thick grass;
crows gather home to the evening wood.
The bright moon falls upon the peak
and shatters a lifetime’s heart.

The mist and cloud were my old sworn lord;
coming back, I am straightway home.
Clouds rise like bolts of white silk;
hills gather round like a lotus.
Deep sleep forgets both dusk and dawn;
dull-witted Chan watches the years pass.
Here is ground where I may set my feet —
no cassock is needed for it.

White hair reflects a withered face;
I hide my form, and for that I have the hills.
The rest of life waits only to dissolve;
one breath, and all returns to ease.
My Chan gone stale, I find it hard to speak;
clouds are deep, and the gate is easily shut.
Perfect penetration enters the flowing water,
murmuring day and night.

Age and idleness suit each other well;
the body wastes, the breath no longer holds.
Seeing and hearing seem all a dream;
rising or sitting, I am suddenly a fool.
Sun and moon go on from dusk to dawn;
bloom and wither, let the seasons have their way.
All that remains is a single thought —
silently entering no-thought.

The night sky clears, and I am glad;
autumn comes, fresh coolness rises.
After rain the forest leaves hang heavy;
wind passes, the ridge clouds lighten.
In still contemplation I see no-self;
hidden in practice, I tire of fame.
Sitting, I watch the moon in the empty realm,
distinct, facing my solitary brightness.

Ten thousand sounds are hushed;
the mind’s source is clear as water.
Censer smoke is fine through the night;
mountain moonlight brightens the window.
Insects rest easy in the grass;
the cloud-sleeping crane is unstartled.
Sitting deep, all thoughts die out —
suddenly I hear the dawn bell.

The heat need not be refused;
coolness surely has its hour.
Clouds fly, the mountain hue falls;
thunder moves, and rain follows.
Grudge not your thin hemp robe its weight;
blame not the autumn wind for being late.
Just sit beneath the pine
and wait for a good wind to blow.

Sitting bolt upright, I closely watch the mind;
its source is not easy to find.
When it stirs, the first omens appear;
where it rises, all is profoundly hidden.
In the still silence, a sound struck in emptiness;
endlessly, the chirp of a fledgling comes forth.
Know that the mark beyond thought
belongs neither to floating nor to sinking.

The gathering of suffering is the cause of birth;
this body of great affliction is hard to dissolve.
What holds it up is only bone;
its movements are not mine to command.
One breath is thin as a thread;
the remnant frame is tiny as dust.
All along it has been but a loan —
in the end, to whom is it dear?

In deep autumn the cold grows heavy;
wrapping the robe close suits me well.
A man grown old, his bones are only stronger;
a pine gone withered, its branches more strange.
The yellow chrysanthemum’s will to live is faint;
white hair is far from worldly feeling.
Sitting alone, all conditions forgotten —
in the vast silence, only I know.

Having crossed the world’s end, still I journey far;
through a hundred Yue mists I count no miles.
Only in peril do I learn how wide the dust-sea is;
at the road’s end I see birth and death as light.
For a while I rest in the light of water and moon;
by chance I walk on ground of lapis lazuli.
The boat that reached the shore is now cast off —
for whom do the upper hall’s bells and drums sound?

In the world the gourd is useless and most things go wrong;
so by the river’s heart I mean to still the workings of my mind.
There is a temple, yet it will not let a monk stay even a while;
having no home, I should race the crane to return.
The cloud of compassion secretly covers the room of the empty-born;
fragrant mist idly soaks a passing traveler’s robe.
A precious raft has hung for a thousand ages over the ford of delusion —
if the swift current would but stop, one would take refuge at once.

From afar I ask Caoxi about the mirror stand;
entering the gate, at one glance our faces break into smiles.
Your body arrives with idle clouds from Mount Lu;
your heart brings the white snow of Mount Yan.
You have journeyed to the end of birth and death, a road beyond the sky;
your greetings spell out all the plum blossoms on the ridge.
If an old friend’s thought could be like yours,
this mind of mine would long ago have turned to ash.

Mist and cloud were always far from the dust of the world;
that I longed for woods and springs, dare I take as true?
Grown old, my mind is like a tree without fire;
what life remains is like a second spring.
Heaven and earth will not permit those who flee to Chan;
the rites and law are not forgotten by one who has left the world.
Only the apes and cranes, companions of the empty hills,
come often in clear dreams to keep this idle body company.

Court and rivers-and-lakes face different skies;
that we meet at all cannot be a bond of this one life.
In office, you wield compassion well;
meeting the world, you set the mind at ease in free and easy Chan.
Your salary alone is fit to pave the ground;
no trace of dust is left to follow you into sleep.
The udumbara bloomed once in three thousand years —
I rejoice it opens again by the Northern Dipper.

Days of rain and cloud, the day will not clear;
at the thatched window I am glad an old friend comes.
Pine blossoms are set aside to brew, brows knit the while;
in the Lotus Society, who would decline a rhapsodist’s gift?
The world’s affairs I only see as finger and horse;
this mind, I will not liken to cold ashes.
Sitting, I hear the flute on the city wall at dusk;
gust after gust of light wind carries the falling plum.

Wind and rain sigh over the boat on the river;
drifting, I have just learned to roam in emptiness.
Waking from a dream, cloud-cranes rest on the pine tops;
at leisure I watch gulls play on the sand.
No need to trouble the north-flying geese with letters;
worldly feeling has long been given to the eastward flow.
Two-thirds of a hundred years are gone;
whatever life remains is only drifting.

The north wind drives the rain and darkens the mountain town;
at the year’s end, at the world’s edge, I am still on a far campaign.
To flee the world I would follow the herd of deer;
on a fearful road the partridge’s cry breaks the heart.
Ten years in the miasmic seas, a lone tumbleweed turning;
one night, frost flowers have grown at both temples.
I whip my horse through mud to a lodging in the wild —
I cannot bear to look back at the evening mist.

Years ago, with a single bowl, we kept long company;
through long nights, robed against the cold, we listened for the dawn bell.
At every meal I think of the realm of Fragrant Accumulation;
walking in meditation, I remember always the Wondrous Peak.
Quietly the fever of miasma melts — the heart holds snow;
silently I count the years by pines my own hands planted.
Sweep the lion seat of the lotus for me,
and wait till I once more raise the teaching beyond words.

How like the Jetavana is this sitting in the banana grove;
I borrow the clear wind to loosen my cares awhile.
Green leaves have often served Huaisu’s brush;
deep shade still covers the sutra-translating porch. (There is the Hall of Sutra Translation and Brush Transmission of Master Fang.)
Out of reverence for life, no morning bowl-round;
to practice samādhi, one should shut the door by day.
I have heard that originally there is not a single thing —
and so all day I sit facing wordlessness.

To inquire into the upward matter, I visited Caoxi;
the matter is clear — from the first, one is not deluded.
In the dawn courtyard the wind rises and blows the green bamboo;
after rain on the spring hills, the staff grows green again.
Only at leisure do I feel all conditions grow still;
only after awakening do I know the ten thousand things are one.
Most intimate is the thing that calls a man awake:
at the fifth watch, a dream broken by a single crowing cock.

That single blow of old divided sage from commoner;
the true, sure pivot dissolves both sight and hearing.
For a thousand autumns incense holds the royal aura;
through all ages the rivers and hills are covered by the cloud of compassion.
In the empty wood the deer still follow the whisk;
the Pure Land’s lotus is already yours.
Leaning on my staff in the spring wind, I stand and linger;
the setting sun’s purple and emerald hang thick with mist.

A whole year of kindred spirits — many a thought it cost;
we meet with joy as the year runs out.
A little boat through a snowy night, a thousand li;
a single branch of Pure Land lotus planted.
The body grown old is only so much lumber;
the days and months ahead are new acquaintance.
Say not that Mount Kuang’s old society is no more —
that it will open again in this place may surely be hoped.

West of Shravasti lies the ancient ground of the Way;
by chance I pass and walk three times around, bowing to the King of Emptiness.
Contemplating mind, I have already entered the mind-only land;
to expound the Dharma I ascend the Hall of Good Dharma.
Fragrant rice can fill the many with contentment;
the ghee is tasted only by the keenest faculties.
The host does not lightly hold it out at once —
if I might come again, I would raise it once more.

The Jetavana opens west of the great river;
a lotus wells from the earth, the best place to rest.
The Buddha-land stands far beyond all marks;
the Dharma-body rises as high as the four heavens.
If I come but briefly, you bid me mount the flower seat;
if I stay long, I should often lean on this staff.
A pity that, visiting, I must say farewell —
I cannot bear to look back, so bleak the heart.

The Plum-Snow Hall opens, and the bones grow purer still;
after the meal I stroll and walk in meditation once.
Fragrance floats in the stone chamber where the flowers first open;
shadows enter the ice-jar, and the moon is doubly bright.
Before the rock of the severed arm an old trace remains;
in the flute that mourns a friend I recall a new sound.
If you would ask today the meaning of the West’s coming —
who can tell the journey of that single shoe?

The Brahma-king’s palace hides in mist and cloud;
outside the gate, red dust and the world’s road stretch far.
The mountain opens a Pure Land from its nine peaks;
monks sit on lotuses of a thousand petals.
Light floats in the stone chamber, where imperial writing remains;
shadows fall on all the heavens, guarding the crimson gauze.
If you would ask the true meaning of the West’s coming —
it is plainly all entrusted to a single cassock.

From afar I bow at the Dharma altar of the famous mountain;
this mind must beg the Patriarch for its peace.
The night moon over the nine peaks whitens a man through;
pine wind from ten thousand gorges chills to the bone.
The lamp of wisdom, nearly out, flares up again;
the robe and bowl are left — to whom shall they be passed?
You should know the day when the heavenly lord took refuge:
the lion’s voice has not yet ceased to speak.

I visit the garden in the suburbs by the Han River’s bank;
it is just like the gathering at Vaiśālī.
We eat our fill of fragrant rice, an offering from heaven;
I delight in subtle words, the songs of Ying.
The level fields were once the model for a monk’s robe;
tall willows newly droop their dew-sprinkled branches.
Wind and rain in the deep night, the mind’s realm still —
cool as though sitting in a pool of lotus flowers.

In a thicket of thorns stands an ancient ground of the Way;
twenty years of toil — for whom was all the busyness?
The hall opens, and dragons and elephants come from the four seas;
three times a day, chanting, they venerate the Dharma King.
Within its bounds the Avataṃsaka realm is perfected;
between the brows the white-hair light shines always.
Looking up in reliance, one has entered the mind-only land —
what need to raise the upward matter again?

A blue lotus rising from water — a heart that dwells in the world;
your carriage and cap are yet as though among woods and hills.
Empty flowers and mirror images — how still the dust;
a lone moon on the cold river, the meaning deeper still.
I think you received your prophecy at Vulture Peak;
in Vaiśālī you must be an old kindred spirit.
We meet, and speak a single word of the un-born;
face to face it is clear — no need to seek.

Alone I climb the high peak, leaning on my staff;
the encroaching empty-green grows bleak and bewildering.
The two eyes of the West’s coming are like a peng’s wings;
eastward, a thousand hills are like horse hooves.
The sheer gorge has long been called a lion’s den;
the empty wood may yet let the elephant king rest.
And now, if I would speak the Dharma of the un-born —
the whisk is barely raised, and the ten thousand forms bow low.

Long ago at Caoxi I formed a noble bond;
since we parted, the Patriarch’s Chan has grown cold and quiet.
When we speak of non-duality, I think of Vimalakīrti;
whenever we talk of the un-born, I remember Danian.
You trust that a magistrate’s office is a manifestation;
who would know that official papers are the true exposition?
The moon before the withered-wood hall on Snow Peak —
tonight, because of you, it wanes and waxes full again.

I have traveled all the rivers and lakes and long grown weary of roaming;
to the green hills I come at last, only when old.
The body has already fallen away, a shadow of empty flowers;
the world’s affairs all follow the flowing water.
In the still silence this idle body takes the clouds for company;
with a sigh, white hair is covered over with snow.
No need to reckon how many years remain:
one thought of the un-born, and vast kalpas rest.

I remember the White Society of Mount Kuang in those days;
this day’s lofty carriage is most worth recalling.
Where one enters, one can at once forget the world’s cares;
arriving here, there is no need at all to knit the brow.
The body is wrapped in cloud-damp from ten thousand gorges;
I sit waiting while the moonlight lags on a thousand peaks.
One night of clear talk becomes a noble trace;
heaven and earth have always prized the heart’s understanding.

I left home as a boy and do not remember the house;
looking back on things past, all is cause for sighing.
Old friends still picture the face of a child;
a withered tree can hardly open its former flowers.
By the river the willow branches droop with morning dew;
before the gate the mountain hues carry the dawn glow.
Only this scene remains, year after year —
no need to ask the past about the passing years.

Twenty years of diligent company in the miasmic land;
again with bowl and staff I roam the many quarters.
To inquire into the mystery I climbed straight to the Golden Wheel’s summit;
entering the room, I still rely on the great Dharma hall.
To go home to the mountain is indeed my wish;
but springs and stones in old age — how could I forget them?
If the years left me are not yet cast into the ditches,
I wait for you to dwell together in the field of stillness.

Your coming suddenly recalls an old friend’s love;
in the end I cannot forget the bond of leaving the world.
At first sight of his bequeathed words, it is as though we faced each other;
thinking back on his talk and laughter, it seems many lives ago.
I know he dwells on a high seat in Tuṣita;
entering straight into bodhi, how should he reckon the journey?
If we meet again as in former days,
would I let a fellow traveler walk a separate road?

From afar you ask after the deer and boars of the empty hills;
your covered cart enters the valley at slanting dusk.
Robes open, we speak softly of what lies in the heart;
brewing tea and burning incense, we sit past midnight.
Glad in the bright moon, the mind is like a mirror;
deeply observing the world’s affairs, they are suddenly as clouds.
If, at the moment, you ask the meaning of the West’s coming —
there is not a single thing I can hand to you.

All my life my tracks have followed former karma;
I am ashamed this bodily frame is not yet free.
One piece of idle mind shows itself wherever I am;
for no reason white hair shifts in the dark.
I know the days to come are all days of subtraction;
who believes that adding years is losing them?
When I look back and go home to the mountain,
I shall sleep on a stone, high-pillowed on ten thousand peaks.

The layman has always manifested a responsive body;
his vajra mind-ground is pure, without a speck of dust.
Long has he awakened to the patient acceptance of the un-born;
dwelling in the world, he is yet as one who has left it.
Vimalakīrti’s Dharma gate is not silence alone;
Layman Pang’s wife and children were not his kin.
His spirit has already entered the lotus treasury —
why ask the great chun tree about the count of kalpas?

Looking back on the five turbidities, the air hangs thick;
the noisy crowd buzzes like mosquitoes in a bowl.
Before a thought flashes, delusion and awakening are cut off;
the moment a mote stands, sage and commoner divide.
The green hills may allow me to hide my clumsiness;
but who can save the burning house from its fire?
I lift my head to the long sky with its pair of blue eyes —
I cannot bear that the whole earth is only floating cloud.

How sad — past things were wanderings in a dream;
flowers in a clouded eye cannot be sought.
The mind’s road is truly like the moon when clouds disperse;
the body, let it be as froth floating on water.
While life holds one breath, three inches still remain;
to enter a thousand peaks in old age is worth ten gains.
From now on my traces in the world are cut —
no more will joy or sorrow come to my brow.

Hidden practice has now fulfilled my first intention;
from of old I have dwelt in mountains and never tired of depth.
Beyond emptiness, let a thousand ranges stand;
can a single speck of dust invade my eyes?
The pine wind ever speaks the Dharma of the un-born;
the flowing water rings long like an ancient lute.
Why trouble to raise the whisk again when entering the room?
The one who knows the tune will take it up at the moment.

In late spring the cold valley’s bamboo shoots put forth buds;
again I see the pine tips slowly come to flower.
A bowl is set out for the noon offering;
the monks all wait to taste the new tea.
Having nothing, I have no heavenly food by magic power;
being rich, I have the adornment of five-colored clouds.
I ask the singers and dancers of Chang’an:
how often have your dreams flown to a mountain household?

Through the three winter months I sit in dry Chan, wrapped in my robe;
gladly I see the spring light, loveliest of all.
A clay pot of wild greens is set as an offering;
the earth stove’s pine fire slowly ceases to smoke.
The green hills under snow open their faces again;
my white hair, kept against the cold, has reached my shoulders.
Fortunate to be a cloud-reclining guest in peaceful times,
I burn incense morning and night to bless the emperor’s years.

Old wanderings seem dimly a former life;
whenever I recall where I went and stayed, I am secretly startled.
These green hills today were that day’s dream;
this White Society now is the old vow.
To answer the moment I use only a steelyard without stars;
to amuse old age I keep only a broken-legged pot.
If you would ask the true meaning of the West’s coming:
the single stream of Caoxi brims full of water.

Dust gathers day by day on the rush mat and incense stand;
old and sick, this world-transcending body finds no room.
Into my dreams the spring’s voice comes clear to the ear;
to my bed the moonlight comes, cold and invading.
The idle mind does not join with any condition;
of pure karma only one true thought remains.
In the end, if you would know where you will come to rest —
the lotus has already tied the bond of lives to come.

A fire of old roots burning a thousand years;
a single-log bridge propping up the way.
No human trace comes or goes;
the hermit is placid, idly still.

The bright moon hangs in the cold sky;
its light pierces to the bottom of the cold pool.
Above and below were always the same;
seen thus, there is no this or that.

Moonlight is pale as water;
the tide is level, cold as emptiness.
A lone boat lies across a wild ford;
the man is in the midst of being and non-being.

We met by chance, and suddenly spoke of parting;
to meet again must be harder still.
Thinking of you, my heart is like snow;
my flying dream is cold at the Ji Gate.

The clear spring is cold as jade;
The fine trees are thick as cloud.
Men here share the joy of ancient peace;
The mind herds with deer and swine.

Ten thousand bamboos ring my old retreat;
the books I read through hang upon the wall.
In dreams I see four eaves, their green complete;
only the moon is left to light the hall.

Seven rolls of the Lotus, a stick of incense;
the long day through I sit cross-legged, at rest.
Beyond the gate, the world’s dust, fathomless hence;
let wheels and hooves run busy — that is best.

The ancestral meaning shows on each grass blade;
spring woods in bloom, bird-song in quiet shade.
At dawn the rain has passed, the hills are rinsed;
on red and white boughs hangs the dew unstained.

Heaven makes iron armor my worn fur cloak;
not for a marquis’s title did I take the field.
Through blazing suns like furnace fires I woke;
my proud bones now are forged and made to yield.

Dew falls upon the orchids, fragrance in the air;
the wind blows over flesh and bone, night turning chill.
Sitting, I feel my mind already white as snow;
the moonlight still I take for frost upon the sill.

We met on Yu Ridge when the day was late and slow;
I thought to break a plum spray — which, I could not say.
Then all at once the autumn wind bore me across the burning sea;
and parting is not as it was on that first day.

Fine trees stand all around, their shadows spread and sparse;
beneath the trees a little cottage lies well hid.
No carriage or horse is heard, all human tracks cut off;
the gate shut, all day long I sit cross-legged alone.

For thirty years and more I have studied laziness and ease;
my whole life sits cut off on Mount Zhurong.
This crane-boned body is light — speak no more of age;
a thousand feet below, look at the pine my own hand planted.

Grown old in the blue hills, I bear my lord's grace;
south of Mount Kuang I lie facing the morning sun.
The Seven Worthies and Five Elders face each other from afar;
the spring's sound speaks deeply of the gate of non-duality.

At Twin Groves I knocked at evening's door,
and old friends clasped sleeves, their hearts at one.
A year apart, we missed the Longhua Assembly;
far off, our letters stopped when the wild geese failed.
Thatched on Mt. Lao, he had gone back to the north sea;
flowers opened on Yu Ridge, toward the southern bough.
Hengyang's land is narrow, and a monk's robe wide;
clouds lie deep on Mt. Kuang, and the staff has moved.
The terrace mirror is empty by nature; he beheld his own self.
Wind and banner suddenly stirred, and men thought of Huineng.
How often the cool moon doubled a clear dream,
one night of autumn wind, face to face with an old friend.
Leaning at his armrest he talked of heaven, gathering hidden meanings;
wielding the brush, he showed his ground and swept away a host of doubts.
Long free of sticky bonds, his mind ever in samādhi;
ympty of all but compassion, his head bowed again and again.
Deep in autumn's fallen leaves, he forgot the weariness of speech;
hearing the bell at midnight, he spoke of the peril of the heart.
Ever he sighed that the dusty world's heart is always bitter,
and that on the Chan terrace the road turns aside.
Hearing the Dharma words, one could only answer, Yes, yes;
the traveler's road urged him on, and so he was ever slow.
The old know that lakes and seas are hard to meet again;
we pledged the karmic hour, then suddenly drifted apart.
The wild goose passes Cold Mountain; autumn's shadow ends.
The horse neighs at Caoxi; the sound of its going is sad.
A foot of white silk carries a letter; a man faces north.
The ācārya hears the news of death, and tears cross his cheeks.
Hard it is to find timber to prop the Dharma gate;
the boat hidden in the sea of enlightenment cannot be chased.
The sleeping butterfly, startled, has only now entered dream;
the dragon soars and coils — where would he go?
To meet him now is already to have passed a thousand kalpas;
to be transformed away needs no word of praise.
Forgetting self, one does not covet the marks of the formless;
beholding emptiness, marvel not at the wonder of form.
Looking back at the peak's colors, the temple at the wood's edge;
I dream of the sound of the tide, poems in the box.
Quiescence never divided going from staying;
his solemn dignity does not alter its old bearing.
My one grief is that Dharma companions must grow few —
to whom now shall the bowl be entrusted, the robe passed on?

Returning to the end, dragons and devas have the Great Master;
from the lecture seat heavenly flowers fell, drifting late.
From the kitchen came the Dharma meal, from the Compassion Palace;
beyond the ridges his grace flowed, and the sage ruler knew it.
Gulls and egrets, and officials, wondered that he played with the world;
sandalwood and the sea treasury had their season of scripture.
Who is his chief disciple, one like Kāśyapa,
to take up and compose the testament I grieve for?

Drop by drop Caoxi weeps for Nanhua;
in those days I myself received the sitting cloth of gauze.
The mind seal was transmitted alone, without a single word;
the golden ground was spread anew with three vehicles.
Forest wind and moon veil the lamp by the bed;
valley rain and mist dissolve the tea after samādhi.
The Dharma's revival in the final age has fallen once more;
lingering by the twin trees, I sigh alone and long.

The prophecy of the Southern School is a thousand years old;
at this way-place he settled once more into Chan.
The Dharma stream lives in the heart, in endless awakening;
the Patriarch has gone, the robe is hidden, never to be transmitted again.
Weeping breaks off the bhikṣu beneath the yellow leaves;
the spirit is buried by the locked gate, beside the white clouds.
He must have left a Bequeathed Teaching Sūtra, equal to the Buddha's;
still, from midstream, one gains the jewel boat.

In that year of the full moon I first tried the Chan of inquiry;
taking refuge at the first ground, I remember Hunan.
The robe was of white felt, and the body ever pure;
the teaching set forth the blue lotus, the tongue containing it again.
Golden plates translated to the end of how many scriptures;
silver hooks wrote out a thousand cases of the Patriarch — (when I had the Master's Surangama Commentary engraved, the Master in turn wrote out for me with his own hand a portrait eulogy of the Patriarch and a biography.)
Who is the true benefactor who has failed the Master?
Three times I sent letters of invitation, and I am ashamed of myself alone.

A thread of spiritual light draws us each to each;
before Eight-Li Village, twenty years ago.
Today I bow to the Master, and his bones are still here;
I know the Master was always an ancient golden immortal.

In the blessed ground of Nanhua his whole body is enshrined;
beneath his feet he still wears the clouds of Wuru.
His staff churned the clear world into mud;
who knows how many men obtained the Dharma?

Weeping over a sealed letter, my heart turns restless;
in vain I write to his hand's traces, pleading the old pledge.
If we had met, we would not have grudged a thousand years hung between;
giving oneself up, one must know, betrays a whole life.
The ancients are praised only for carrying their shoes away;
at a deathbed, who understands how to bray like a donkey?
Bleak is the traveler's inn in the last snows of winter;
the specks on my sleeve cling to me as if they had feeling.

Tears fall to the edge of heaven, sent to my brothers;
the late Master's testament is all too clear.
Brows and beard of the Five Elders can be pictured;
waist and spine of three ridges come of themselves.
Red leaves: the blood of both eyes of a man of this land.
White clouds: a disciple's single-minded banner.
Here I receive the earnest meaning;
why need we talk grandly of death and life?
